-Flame in Freefall combines four NASA microgravity combustion datasets into a single dashboard and a set of models:
+---
 
-- An early-warning smoke detector trained only on clean cabin air, using one Isolation Forest per sensor.
-- A classifier that identifies which of five spacecraft materials is overheating from aerosol sensor signatures.
-- Burn-time and flame-outcome models trained on FLEX droplet tests, used by an interactive flame simulator.
-- Analytics views for FLEX, BASS-II and Saffire-I, with each data source labelled by provenance.
-- A sensor and telemetry integration layer that normalises NASA PSI tables and time series (SAME-R, Saffire-III and others) into validated, timestamped packets with full provenance, which the dashboard reads directly.
+## 1. Problem formulation
 
-## Team
+In microgravity there is no buoyant plume, so smoke from an overheating material stays near the source and spreads with cabin ventilation instead of rising to a ceiling detector. Overheated polymers and fabrics pyrolyse and release aerosol **before** open flame. That pre-ignition window is where detection is most valuable. The NASA SAME experiment measured this window on the ISS in 2010.
 
