-## Limitations
+Greedy forward selection (`reports/sensor_sensitivity/greedy_selection_loto.csv`) gives these material macro-F1 values: plume temperature 0.59 (25/38) → + ISS obscuration 0.78 (29/38) → + P-Trak 0.77 → + Ionisation B 0.79 (30/38). With all 8 channels it drops to 0.69 (27/38) because the extra channels add noise for the classifier. Every greedy subset keeps the 3 s median detection latency. The two-channel subset (plume temperature + ISS obscuration) needs 8 B/s of telemetry at 1 Hz float32 instead of 32 B/s. Because the subset was chosen on the same CV results, its F1 is optimistic (see §5). Figures (300 DPI PNG and PDF): `reports/sensor_sensitivity/figures/latency_per_sensor_loto.*` and `greedy_selection_loto.*`.
 
-- The SAME set is small: 41 tests and five materials.
-- The detector's lead time comes mostly from the gas plume temperature channel, which triggered the alert in all 41 tests. Using smoke sensors alone, the median lead over the scatter alarm falls to about 1 s. A temperature sensor near likely ignition sources is therefore the main hardware recommendation.
-- SAME heats samples to pyrolysis and does not include open flaming, so the detector covers the pre-ignition smoke phase only.
-- Three SAME tests have uncertain material labels. They are excluded from classifier training and reported separately.
-- The 69 entries in the variable registry are research variables reported by the experiments. They are not a verified inventory of physical sensor hardware.
